---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import GuideCta from '../components/GuideCta.astro';
import { SCALES } from '../scripts/gpa';
import { PERCENT_BANDS } from '../scripts/gpa-tools';
const rows = PERCENT_BANDS.map((b) => [b.letter, b.label, SCALES.plus[b.letter].toFixed(1), SCALES.p433[b.letter].toFixed(2)]);
---
<PageLayout type="article" enOnly published="2026-10-23" modified="2026-11-03" title="GPA Scale: 4.0 Grade Chart with Percent & Letter Grades" description="The 4.0 GPA scale in one chart: every letter grade and percent with its grade points, plus a percent-to-GPA converter and the 4.33 and 5.0 scales explained." path="/gpa-scale-chart/" heading="GPA Scale and Chart: Letter Grade, Percent and Points">
  <p>On the standard 4.0 GPA scale, an A is worth 4.0, a B is 3.0, a C is 2.0, a D is 1.0 and an F is 0. Use this chart to convert a letter grade or percentage to grade points. Percent ranges vary by school, so treat these as one widely used version and check your handbook.</p>

  <div class="card not-prose mt-6 p-5" data-percent>
    <label class="block"><span class="lbl">Percent to GPA: enter your percentage</span><input class="field num" type="text" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off" maxlength="5" placeholder="e.g. 90" /></label>
    <p class="m-0 mt-3 min-h-[52px] text-[15px] text-[var(--ink)]" aria-live="polite" data-out>Your result appears here as you type.</p>
  </div>
  <h2>GPA conversion chart</h2>

  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="tbl">
      <caption class="sr-only">Letter grade, percentage and GPA points</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Letter</th><th scope="col">Percent</th><th scope="col">4.0 scale</th><th scope="col">4.33 scale</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{rows.map((r) => <tr><td>{r[0]}</td><td class="num">{r[1]}</td><td class="num">{r[2]}</td><td class="num">{r[3]}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Which scale does my school use?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Plus/minus 4.0:</strong> the table's 4.0 column. Some schools also use a D- worth 0.7, which is not shown.</li>
    <li><strong>Letters only:</strong> A = 4.0, B = 3.0, C = 2.0, D = 1.0, F = 0, with no plus or minus. The <a href="https://bigfuture.collegeboard.org/plan-for-college/college-basics/how-to-convert-gpa-4.0-scale" rel="noopener">College Board's guide</a> shows this version, with 90–100 as an A.</li>
    <li><strong>4.33:</strong> an A+ is worth 4.33, and plus and minus grades move by about a third of a point.</li>
    <li><strong>Weighted 5.0:</strong> Honors and AP classes earn bonus points, so an A in an AP class can count as 5.0. See the <a href="/ap-honors-gpa-weights/">weighted GPA scale</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How do I convert a 5.0 GPA to a 4.0 scale?</h2>
  <p>You cannot do it accurately by multiplying, because a 5.0 scale usually reflects bonus points for some classes and not others. Recalculate using each class's unweighted grade points instead. The <a href="/weighted-vs-unweighted-gpa/">weighted vs unweighted guide</a> shows why.</p>
  <p>Our <a href="/#calculator">GPA calculator</a> supports all three scales.</p>

  <h2>From grade points to GPA</h2>
  <p>Once you have grade points for each class, multiply by credits, add, and divide by total credits. Walk through it in <a href="/how-to-calculate-gpa/">how to calculate your GPA</a>. For Honors and AP bonus points, see <a href="/ap-honors-gpa-weights/">AP and Honors weights</a>.</p>
  <GuideCta current="/gpa-scale-chart/" />
</PageLayout>
<script>
  import { percentToGpa } from '../scripts/gpa-tools';
  const box = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-percent]')!;
  const input = box.querySelector('input')!;
  const out = box.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-out]')!;
  input.addEventListener('input', () => {
    const raw = input.value.trim().replace('%', '');
    if (!raw) { out.textContent = 'Your result appears here as you type.'; return; }
    const r = /^\d*\.?\d+$/.test(raw) ? percentToGpa(Number(raw)) : null;
    out.textContent = r ? `${r.letter} = ${r.points.toFixed(1)} on the 4.0 scale, ${r.points433.toFixed(2)} on the 4.33 scale.` : 'Enter a percentage from 0 to 100.';
  });
</script>
